%N A402161 a(n) is the least k >= 1 such that the n consecutive integers k, k+1, ..., k+n-1 cannot be cut into an initial block and a final block, both nonempty, with coprime sums; a(n) = 0 if there is no such k.
%C A402161 A cut after the q-th term (1 <= q <= n-1) divides the run k, k+1, ..., k+n-1 into its first q terms and its last n-q terms; the cut is coprime if the sums of the two blocks have greatest common divisor 1. So a(n) is the least k for which no cut of the run is coprime. For example, the run 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 has no coprime cut, and each of the runs starting at 1, 2, 3 has one, so a(5) = 4 (see the Example section). A term a(n) = 0 means that every run of n consecutive positive integers has a coprime cut.
%C A402161 Theorem (proved in the notes file): a(n) = 0 if and only if n is 2, 3, 4, 18 or twice a Fermat prime, that is, twice a prime among the Fermat numbers F_j = 2^(2^j) + 1, j >= 0 (A019434). The five known Fermat primes 3, 5, 17, 257, 65537 give n = 6, 10, 34, 514, 131074, so nine indices n with a(n) = 0 are known: 2, 3, 4, 6, 10, 18, 34, 514, 131074. There is a tenth one if and only if there is a sixth Fermat prime. Since F_5, ..., F_32 are known to be composite (see the Crandall, Mayer and Papadopoulos link), a tenth one would be at least 2*F_33, a number with 2585827974 decimal digits. The exceptional index 18 = 2*9 comes from 9 = 2^3 + 1, the only prime power of the form 2^h + 1 with h >= 1 that is not a prime (cf. A365640).
%C A402161 The cut after q terms of the run starting at k is coprime if and only if gcd(n*(2*k+n-1)/2, q*(2*k+q-1)/2) = 1 (the first number is the sum of the whole run, the second is the sum of its first q terms), and an odd prime that divides both of these numbers divides n, q or n-q, so it is at most n. Hence whether a run has a coprime cut depends only on k modulo the product of the primes up to n, which is A034386(n). So every term can be found by a finite search, and a positive term is less than this product.
%C A402161 The positive terms are in fact much smaller than A034386(n), whose logarithm is asymptotic to n by the prime number theorem: it is proved in the notes file that there is a constant c with log(a(n)) < c*n/log(n) for all sufficiently large n with a(n) > 0 (by Selberg's sieve). In the other direction, it is proved there that the positive terms eventually exceed every fixed power of n (by an elementary count), and that a(n) > exp(n^(1/5)) for all sufficiently large n with a(n) > 0 (by the sieve of Diamond, Halberstam and Richert, in the form stated by Martin). For 20 <= n <= 813 with a(n) > 0, the ratio log(a(n))/(n/log(n)) lies between 0.44 and 1.83. The largest term with n <= 813 is a(774), which has 92 digits.
%C A402161 The following patterns hold for all n <= 813 and are not proved in general. From n = 54 on, every record value (a term larger than all earlier terms) occurs at an index n == 6 (mod 12). For n = 54, 66, 78, ..., 798 the term a(n) is larger than the five terms before it and the five terms after it. If a(n) > 0, then 3 divides the sum 2*a(n)+n-1 of the first and the last term of the run exactly when 3 does not divide n. For n <= 813, the only n with 0 < a(n) < n are 5, 8, 20.
%C A402161 The condition differs from those of two classical problems about runs of consecutive integers: runs in which every term has a common factor greater than 1 with some other term (Pillai, Brauer; see A090318 and the Gassko link), and Erdős-Woods intervals, in which every term has a common factor greater than 1 with the first or with the last term (A059756, A059757; see the Dowe link). The run 4, ..., 8 has no coprime cut, although 7 is coprime to each of the other terms and 5 is coprime to both 4 and 8. The run 2184, ..., 2200 satisfies both classical conditions and has a coprime cut after its third term (the sums are 6555 and 30709). See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
