%F A402161 a(n) = 0 if and only if n is in {2, 3, 4, 18} or n is twice a term of A019434.
%F A402161 a(n) < A034386(n) if a(n) > 0.
%F A402161 There is a constant c such that exp(n^(1/5)) < a(n) < exp(c*n/log(n)) for all sufficiently large n with a(n) > 0.
%e A402161 For n = 5, the pairs (x, y) of sums of the two blocks, for each start k and each cut after q terms:
%e A402161   k   run          q=1      q=2      q=3       q=4
%e A402161   1   1,2,3,4,5    (1,14)   (3,12)   (6,9)     (10,5)
%e A402161   2   2,3,4,5,6    (2,18)   (5,15)   (9,11)    (14,6)
%e A402161   3   3,4,5,6,7    (3,22)   (7,18)   (12,13)   (18,7)
%e A402161   4   4,5,6,7,8    (4,26)   (9,21)   (15,15)   (22,8)
%e A402161 The first three runs have coprime cuts, for example (1,14), (9,11) and (3,22). In the last run the four greatest common divisors are 2, 3, 15, 2. Hence a(5) = 4.
%e A402161 For n = 6, the cut after two terms has sums 2*k+1 and 4*k+14, whose greatest common divisor is gcd(2*k+1, 3); so this cut is coprime unless k == 1 (mod 3). The cut after four terms has sums 4*k+6 and 2*k+9, whose greatest common divisor is gcd(2*k+9, 3); so this cut is coprime unless k == 0 (mod 3). Since k cannot be both 1 and 0 modulo 3, one of these two cuts is always coprime, and a(6) = 0.
%o A402161 (Python)
%o A402161 from itertools import count
%o A402161 from math import gcd, isqrt
%o A402161 for n in range(2, 54):
%o A402161     m = n // 2
%o A402161     # Zero classification: see the Comments section.
%o A402161     if n <= 4 or n == 18 or (n % 2 == 0 and m >= 3 and
%o A402161         (m-1) & (m-2) == 0 and all(m % d for d in range(2, isqrt(m)+1))):
%o A402161         print(0)
%o A402161         continue
%o A402161     for k in count(1):
%o A402161         total = n * (2*k+n-1) // 2
%o A402161         if all(gcd(total, q*(2*k+q-1)//2) > 1 for q in range(1, n)):
%o A402161             print(k)
%o A402161             break
